---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import D from "../data/diaspora.json";
import register from "../data/register.json";
const towns = D.towns.map((t) => ({ ...t, falco: +t.falco_records || 0,
  zamp: +t.zampiello || 0, ann: +t.annecchino || 0 }))
  .sort((a, b) => (b.falco + b.zamp + b.ann) - (a.falco + a.zamp + a.ann));
const max = Math.max(...towns.map((t) => t.falco + t.zamp + t.ann), 1);
const tot = towns.reduce((a, t) => a + t.falco + t.zamp + t.ann, 0);
---
<Base title="Where the name went"
      eyebrow="Falco, Zampiello and Annecchino across the Valle Caudina"
      dek="Five towns within a day's walk hold thousands of records under these three surnames. Almost none of them are this family — and knowing which is the whole problem."
      >

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>A surname is not a family, and in this valley it is barely even a clue.</strong> The
    Antenati indexes hold <strong>{tot.toLocaleString()} records</strong> under Falco, Zampiello and
    Annecchino across five neighbouring comuni. <strong>This archive can attach fewer than nine
    hundred of them to anybody.</strong></p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.6rem">The bars below are why <a href={u("/people")}>the
    people page</a> keeps a «★ my line» filter and why it was built on <em>attachment</em> rather than
    name-matching: name-matching alone would have pulled in every Pasquale Falco in the valley.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="dbars">
    {towns.map((t) => (
      <div class="row">
        <div class="nm"><strong>{t.town}</strong></div>
        <div class="bwrap">
          {t.falco ? <div class="bar f" style={`width:${(t.falco / max) * 100}%`} title={`Falco ${t.falco}`}></div> : null}
          {t.zamp ? <div class="bar z" style={`width:${(t.zamp / max) * 100}%`} title={`Zampiello ${t.zamp}`}></div> : null}
          {t.ann ? <div class="bar a" style={`width:${(t.ann / max) * 100}%`} title={`Annecchino ${t.ann}`}></div> : null}
          <span class="v mono">{(t.falco + t.zamp + t.ann).toLocaleString()}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
    <p class="key small"><span class="sw f"></span> Falco &nbsp; <span class="sw z"></span> Zampiello
      &nbsp; <span class="sw a"></span> Annecchino</p>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Falco</th><th>Zampiello</th><th>Annecchino</th><th>In-laws shared with Arienzo</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {towns.map((t) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{t.town}</strong></td>
          <td class="mono small">{t.falco || ""}</td>
          <td class="mono small">{t.zamp || ""}</td>
          <td class="mono small">{t.ann || ""}</td>
          <td class="small">{t.shared_inlaws_with_arienzo}</td>
          <td class="small">{t.note}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.2rem 0;border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <p><strong>Paolisi is the one to sit with.</strong> It holds the largest Falco cluster in the
    valley — and <strong>a Francesco Falco «Casalis Paulisii» came from there to Arienzo in
    1714</strong>, a generation before Matteo. <strong>Nothing joins him to this line</strong>, and
    the archive does not claim it. But if the Falco of Arienzo came from anywhere, Paolisi is where
    the next search goes.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Moiano holds all three surnames at once</strong> — 310 Falco,
    104 Zampiello, 215 Annecchino — and shares the Morgillo with Arienzo. <strong>It has never been
    examined.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the name went after Italy</h2>
  <p><strong>Brisbane, and almost nowhere else this archive can see.</strong> Generation eight
  crossed in the twentieth century and <a href={u("/graves")}>lies at Nudgee</a>. There is no evidence
  here of a Falco diaspora to the Americas, to Argentina or to northern Europe — <strong>which is not
  the same as saying there wasn't one.</strong> It means nobody has looked, and the sources that
  would show it (passenger lists, naturalisations, parish books outside Campania) are
  <a href={u("/open-questions")}>on the list of things not yet read</a>.</p>

<style is:global>
  .dbars{margin:1rem 0 1.4rem;font-family:var(--sans)}
  .dbars .row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:9rem 1fr;gap:.7rem;align-items:center;
              padding:.3rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
  .dbars .bwrap{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:2px}
  .dbars .bar{height:.6rem;border-radius:1px;min-width:2px}
  .dbars .bar.f{background:var(--accent)}
  .dbars .bar.z{background:var(--terra)}
  .dbars .bar.a{background:var(--ochre)}
  .dbars .v{font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-2);margin-left:.5rem}
  .dbars .key{margin-top:.7rem}
  .dbars .sw{display:inline-block;width:.7rem;height:.55rem;border-radius:2px;vertical-align:middle}
  .dbars .sw.f{background:var(--accent)} .dbars .sw.z{background:var(--terra)} .dbars .sw.a{background:var(--ochre)}
</style>
</Base>
